xgis 1

// NOAA S-102 LIVE bathymetry (#1366 INC-4) — streams a REAL NOAA S-102 cell straight
// from the NOAA Open-Data S3 bucket (noaa-s102-pds), read as the S-100 HDF5 standard IN
// PLACE (ADR-0010, no transcode). This is the first demo drawing a PROJECTED coverage:
// the cell's grid is EPSG:32618 (WGS 84 / UTM zone 18N) with a 16 m spacing and a metre
// origin, NOT degrees.
//
// That is the whole point of #1366. Until INC-3 the drape walked a lon/lat rectangle, so
// a UTM cell would have been placed as if metres were degrees — off the planet, not off
// by a little — and the reader refused it outright rather than mis-render. The drape now
// reprojects its mesh NODES through the cell's own CRS on the CPU (`coverageMeshNodes`),
// so the grid lands in Chesapeake Bay where it belongs.
//
// CORS: like the S-111 bucket, noaa-s102-pds serves HTTP-Range but sets no
// Access-Control-Allow-Origin, so the browser reaches it through the general `/noaa/
// <bucket>/<key>` dev-proxy bridge (a CORS-open Worker in prod).
//
// Cell choice is no longer hard-coded (#1453) — the catalogue picks whatever the viewport
// covers. S-102 keys are STABLE (a cell is re-issued in place when its survey updates — the
// content rolls, the key does not), unlike the S-111 rolling date tree that needs a `latest`
// resolver, so the catalogue's hrefs do not rot either.

// Esri World Imagery satellite basemap (same source the S-111 live demo uses), so the
// bathymetry draws over real nautical imagery. CORS `*`.
source basemap {
  type: raster
  url: "https://server.arcgisonline.com/ArcGIS/rest/services/World_Imagery/MapServer/tile/{z}/{y}/{x}"
}

// The `url:` names a CATALOGUE of cells, not one cell (#1453) — and unlike S-111, this one is
// NOAA'S OWN. `noaa-s102-pds/ed3.0.0/_CATALOG/CATALOG.XML` is an IHO S-100 Exchange Catalogue
// listing all 4313 published cells with their bounding boxes; nothing here invents that table.
// The proxy translates it to STAC server-side so the browser reads ~1 MB of JSON instead of
// 19 MB of XML for what is only a bbox lookup — the cells themselves are still read IN PLACE
// as HDF5 over Range (ADR-0010).
//
// So panning works: the ENGINE intersects the viewport with those envelopes on each move-end
// and loads the cells that cover it. Before this the demo hard-coded ONE cell key, and moving
// off Chesapeake Bay simply showed nothing — there was no machinery to load a neighbour, and
// unlike S-111 this demo never even had the app-side kind.
//
// NOT FOR NAVIGATION: NOAA marks this edition test-and-evaluation, and each catalogue item
// carries that flag through.
source bathy {
  type: coverage
  url: "/noaa-s102/catalog.json"
}

layer imagery {
  source: basemap
}

// Depth is positive-down in S-102, so the ramp runs shallow→deep across the cell's range.
// The Chesapeake approach is shallow: 0-30 m covers it without flattening the gradient.
layer depth {
  source: bathy
  ramp: "bathymetry"
  range: [0, 30]
  | opacity-80
}

// Sounding NUMERALS over the ramp (#1366 INC-5) — the reading a colour ramp cannot give.
// The ramp shows the seabed's shape; these give the metres, straight off the ORIGINAL grid
// (`valueAt`, nearest-cell, never interpolated), so a numeral can only show a depth that is
// actually in the cell. `.depth` is the S-102 band name, exactly as a feature property.
//
// Candidates are picked on a GRID-anchored lattice — every stride'th cell, the stride a power
// of two derived from the current pixels-per-cell. That holds a roughly constant on-screen
// density (the chart behaviour) with no zoom rule to author, and it keeps the chosen SET from
// changing when the camera only rotates or pitches. Zoom in past one cell per lattice step and
// the count falls toward one numeral per cell, which is the honest limit: a gridded product
// holds no more soundings than it holds cells.
//
// `{.depth:.1f}` is the label text-format mini-language — `{<expr>:<spec>}` with the familiar
// printf/Python spec `[fill][align][sign][#][0][width][grouping][.precision][type]`, plus GIS
// types (`dms`, `mgrs`, …) and a `;<locale>` tail. One decimal is what a metric chart carries;
// the raw band is f32 to the millimetre, which is precision the survey does not have.
layer soundings {
  source: bathy
  | label-["{.depth:.1f}"] label-color-#fff label-size-11
    label-halo-1 label-halo-color-#000
}
